---
import CalcPage from '../../components/CalcPage.astro';
import Field from '../../components/Field.astro';
---

<CalcPage
  title="Specific gravity"
  kicker="Concentration"
  lede="The bridge between weight and volume. When a formula gives you milliliters but your balance measures grams, specific gravity is what gets you across."
>
  <form slot="form" id="calc">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <Field
        label="Specific gravity"
        name="sg"
        placeholder="1.25"
        hint="No units — it is a ratio."
      />
      <Field label="Mass (g)" name="mass" placeholder="leave blank" />
      <Field label="Volume (mL)" name="volume" placeholder="100" />
    </div>

    <p class="hint" style="margin-top:-0.6rem">
      Fill in two boxes and leave the third empty.
    </p>

    <div class="field-actions">
      <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Solve</button>
      <button class="btn btn--quiet" type="reset">Clear</button>
    </div>

    <div class="field-actions" style="margin-top:0.9rem">
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"sg":"1.25","mass":"","volume":"100"}'
      >
        Example: glycerin
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"sg":"0.816","mass":"500","volume":""}'
      >
        Example: alcohol by weight
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"sg":"","mass":"60","volume":"50"}'
      >
        Example: find the sg
      </button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <div slot="concept">
    <h2>A number with no units</h2>
    <p>
      Specific gravity compares the density of your substance to the density of
      water at the same temperature:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">           density of the substance
   sg  =  ─────────────────────────
             density of water</pre>

    <p>
      Because both the top and the bottom are densities, the units cancel and
      you are left with a bare number. That is not a mistake — it is the whole
      point. It makes specific gravity a pure scaling factor.
    </p>

    <h3>Why it collapses to mass over volume</h3>
    <p>
      Water has a density of almost exactly <strong>1 g/mL</strong>. Dividing
      by 1 changes nothing, so in practice:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">   sg  =  mass in grams  ÷  volume in milliliters</pre>

    <p>
      This is why a substance with an sg of 1.25 weighs 1.25 times what the
      same volume of water would. 100 mL of it weighs 125 g.
    </p>

    <h3>Reading the number</h3>
    <p>
      Above 1 means denser than water and it sinks — glycerin, syrup, most
      concentrated acids. Below 1 means lighter than water and it floats —
      alcohol, most oils. Petrolatum sits just under 1, which is worth
      remembering for ointment work.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Temperature is part of the definition.</strong> Densities shift
        with heat, so an official specific gravity is quoted at a stated
        temperature, usually 25&nbsp;°C. In coursework you can normally ignore
        this, but it is the reason the value is never quite exact.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</CalcPage>

<script>
  import { setupCalculator } from '../../scripts/calc.js';
  import { solveSpecificGravity } from '../../scripts/solvers/specific-gravity.js';

  setupCalculator({
    form: '#calc',
    solve: solveSpecificGravity,
  });
</script>
